LAST_SUCCESS=Task 117 M2 step 2 DONE — backend REGISTRY (the decoupling seam) + software H.264 via openh264. Registry: CodecBackend trait (name/kind/priority/supports/open_*), priority dispatch + fall-to-next-on-Err, Preferences{no_hardware,require_hardware}. Adding a backend (HW lane, or oxideav when ready) = 1 file + 1 line in default_registry(). openh264 (Cisco BSD-2, desktop-only, encodes+decodes): H.264 MAE 1.64, PTS preserved+monotonic, teeth-checked (drop-PTS and kind=Hardware both caught). Crate tests h264 3/3 roundtrip 5/5 playback 5/5; clippy clean; host desktop + Android cross-build green.
NOTES=Registry bakes in the oxideav spike lesson: fallback keys on Err, not on frames — a backend must Err from open_* if it won't work. KNOWN LIMIT: openh264 decoder gives no per-frame PTS, so FIFO pairing — valid ONLY without B-frames (our encoder is CameraVideoRealTime = no B-frames). Host dep enables only `libvpx` (not openh264) — nothing consumes H.264 in the host yet, so no Cisco C built into shipped host. NEXT = step 2b: B-frame reorder buffer + feed a real MP4 (h264_mp4toannexb: MP4 stores avcC length-prefixed NALs, decoders want Annex-B, ~100 lines no crate) + wire H.264 into the host (extend video::Codec, enable openh264 feature) = the 1b-equivalent for H.264. THEN HW backends (VAAPI on popos first — real /dev/dri + iHD driver). Removed scratch bench_sw.rs (would break bare cargo test).
